          <h2 id="buoyancy-driven-flow-title">
            Buoyancy-driven flow at mid-ocean ridges
          </h2>
          <div class="project-description">
            <p>
              Mid-ocean ridge are tectonic plate boundaries where most of magmatism on Earth occurs. 
              In the classical view, the partially molten region extends symmetrically beneath the ridge axis. Observations, instead, show significant degree of asymmetry at various mid-ocean ridges.
              Here, we developed a mid-ocean ridge model to investigate buoyancy-driven flow due to
              porosity, composition, and temperature. Right: simulation showing
              development of asymmetry beneath the ridge axis and small-scale
              convection at the lithosphere-asthenosphere boundary. Top panel
              represents melt fraction (porosity); bottom panel represents bulk
              density relative to reference solid mantle.
            </p>
          </div>
